<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhu Xi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1130">1130</a>–<a href="/years/1200">1200</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zhu Xi was a Chinese philosopher of the Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> and the leading figure of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-confucianism">Neo-Confucianism</a>. His synthesis of Confucian ethics and metaphysics, and his commentaries on the Four Books, later became the basis of the imperial civil service examinations. His thought became the intellectual foundation of East Asian governance and social organisation for six centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhu-xi.jpg" alt="Zhu Xi" width="320" height="360" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 向史公哲曰 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%AF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%AF%E9%9D%A0%E7%9A%84%E6%9C%B1%E7%86%B9%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zhu Xi was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> on 18 October 1130, where his father worked as subprefectural sheriff. His family originated in Wuyuan County, Huizhou, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangxi">Jiangxi</a> province. After his father was forced from office in 1140 for opposing the government's appeasement policy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jurchens">Jurchen</a>, Zhu Xi was taught at home by him. When his father died in 1143, he studied with his father's friends Hu Xian, Liu Zihui and Liu Mianzhi. In 1148, at the age of 19, he passed the imperial examination and became a presented scholar (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinshi">jinshi</a>). From 1153 he studied under Li Tong, who followed the Neo-Confucian tradition of Cheng Hao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheng-yi-philosopher">Cheng Yi</a>, and he became Li's formal student in 1160.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Zhu Xi's first official post was Subprefectural Registrar of Tong'an, from 1153 to 1156. He then held no office until 1179, when he was appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prefect">Prefect</a> of Nankang Military District and revived the White Deer Grotto Academy. Three years later he was demoted for attacking the incompetence and corruption of some influential officials. After his last dismissal he was accused of numerous crimes and a petition was made for his execution, with much of the opposition led by the Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-tuozhou">Han Tuozhou</a>, a political rival. He wrote, compiled and edited almost a hundred books and taught groups of students, many of whom studied under him for years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Rather than concentrating on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/i-ching">I Ching</a> as other Neo-Confucians did, Zhu Xi emphasised the Four Books: the Great Learning, the Doctrine of the Mean, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analects">Analects</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a>. His commentaries on them were not widely recognised in his time but later became the standard ones, and from 1313 to 1905 they formed the basis of the civil service examinations. He held that all things arise from the union of qi, a vital or material force, and li, a rational principle, and that the source and sum of li is the taiji, the Supreme Ultimate. Qi obscures each person's perfect moral nature, so moral cultivation means clearing the qi. He advocated the investigation of things and practised a daily meditation called jingzuo.</p><p>He died on 23 April 1200. Almost a thousand people attended his funeral, although his teachings had been severely attacked. In 1208 Emperor Ningzong of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> rehabilitated him, and in 1241 a memorial tablet was placed to him in the Confucian Temple at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qufu">Qufu</a>. His teaching became influential in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where it is called Shushigaku, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, where it became an orthodoxy known as Jujahak, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhu Xi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Neo-Confucian synthesis of <b>Zhu Xi</b> became the orthodox basis of the civil service examinations from 1313 and guided East Asian government and social organisation for the following six centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1241, under Emperor Lizong, <b>Zhu Xi</b>'s Four Books and his commentary became required study for the examinations.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Song dynasty the philosopher <b>Zhu Xi</b>, who lived from 1130 to 1200, selected four texts as the core of learning: the Analects, the Mencius, and two chapters of the Book of Rites, the Great Learning and the Doctrine of the Mean.</blockquote><a href="/religion/confucianism">Confucianism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The Song dynasty scholar <b>Zhu Xi</b> likewise called it the records of Confucius's first- and second-generation pupils.</blockquote><a href="/work/analects">Analects · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Zhu Xi</b>, in the 12th century, argued that it was primarily a work of divination usable for moral self-cultivation.</blockquote><a href="/work/i-ching">I Ching · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The examinations tested the Confucian classics, above all the Four Books outlined by <b>Zhu Xi</b>, and from 1487 the intricate “eight-legged essay”.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ming-china">Social Hierarchy of Ming China</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Youxi County, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">People's Republic of China</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=26.17,118.19&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Youxi County on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zhu Xi"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4861 5326h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4861 5326h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=26.17,118.19&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Do not bother the gods with too many prayers. Do not make allowances for your own shortcomings. Do not seek to know what has not yet come to pass.&#8221;</p><footer>The Little Learning (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…it was quite a joke to see how accurately he reproduced my coarse ugly face and my vulgar rustic turn of mind…&#8221;</p><footer>Testimonial for the portrait painter Kuo Kung-chen (in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;When God is about to send down calamities upon us, He first raises up the hero whose genius shall finally prevail against those calamities.&#8221;</p><footer>A saying quoted in Giles, A History of Chinese Literature &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1901 &#183; as a later writer quotes him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/43711/pg43711-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;And I thought how much I should like to bring back with me portraits of the various great and good, but unknown, men I might be fortunate enough to meet with on the way.&#8221;</p><footer>Testimonial for the portrait painter Kuo Kung-chen (in Giles, Gems of Chinese Literature) &#183; tr. Herbert A. Giles, 1923 &#183; <a href="https://gutenberg.net.au/ebooks08/0800161.txt" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg Australia</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
